## Problem

linkedom (htmlparser2) does not run the HTML tree builder's table rules. A `<thead>`, `<tbody>`, `<tfoot>`, `<tr>`, `<td>`, `<th>` or `<caption>` written inside a cell stays where it is written. htmlparser2 closes a cell only when a `<tr>` or `<td>` is its direct child, so `<td>a<th>b` nests the `<th>` inside the `<td>`. Browsers close the cell there.

As a result, such a cell's text held the rows written in it, and those same rows were also rows of the table. For example, `<td>a<thead><tr><td>x</td><td>y</td></tr></thead></td>` gave the cell `a x y`.

## Fix

Both steps are in `packages/extract-tf/src/markdown.ts`. `convert` runs them right after parsing, when the document has a `<table>`.

**`misnestedTables`.** One iterative pass over the document finds the tables that linkedom built differently from a browser's parser:
- table structure inside a cell or caption;
- anything else where a row group, row or cell belongs, such as a `<div>` around rows, a caption in a `<tbody>`, or a cell directly in a `<tbody>`;
- non-whitespace text there.

A `<form>` around rows passes through, because old pages do this. `<script>`, `<style>`, `<template>` and `<input>` are allowed there. The pass does not search inside `<template>` or `<colgroup>`.

**`rebuildTable`.** Each table found is rebuilt by the tree builder's table, row group, row, cell and caption rules, using a loop of enter and exit steps:
- a structural element closes the cell, row or group it ends;
- an element still open in a cell that has closed ends there;
- text and elements at the table's own level move before the table;
- a `<table>` at that level ends the table, and it and what follows come after the table, with rows and cells read as their content alone;
- a table nested in a cell, a `<template>`, a `<script>` and a `<style>` stay whole;
- attributes such as colspan, rowspan and `data-w2l-*` are kept on the copied elements.

`EXTRACTOR_VERSION` is now `extract-tf/10`; the version test's digest has not changed. CHANGELOG and README are updated.

## Checks (commit bf5de18)

- **Failing tests first.** I added two tests to `packages/extract-tf/test/tables.test.ts` and one to `markdown.test.ts`, each with the result Chromium gives for the same HTML. They cover:
  - structure in cells, including inside a `<span>` or `<div>`;
  - `<th>` in a `<td>`;
  - a caption in a cell;
  - text after the structure, which moves before the table;
  - `<template>` rows (an Alpine-style table);
  - a `<table>` at table level;
  - a cell 4,000 elements deep;
  - a nested table that stays the cell's text.

  The reviewer confirmed the tests fail on 76530f0.
- **Comparison with Chromium on random tables with structure written in cells.** Each run compares the cells, the caption and the text before the table:

  | Run | Tables | Mismatches after | Mismatches on 76530f0 |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | 1 | 1,905 | 0 | 1,782 |
  | 2 | 1,939 | 0 | 1,818 |
  | 3 | 1,765 | 0 | 1,765 |

  In runs 2 and 3, a `<td>` or `<tr>` written in a cell is wrapped in a `<span>` or `<div>`. Without that wrapping, 545 of 1,939 tables still differ; see the known gap below. No table that matched Chromium on 76530f0 stops matching.
- **Sloppy markup.** I generated 20,000 random tables with unclosed cells, comments and `<form>` wrappers. 4,718 now give different output, and all 4,718 match Chromium; none of them matched on 76530f0. The most common case is an unclosed `<td>` followed by a `<th>`.
- **Unchanged pages.** 120 locally captured pages (`.w2l/`, read locally, only counts kept) contain 298 tables. Their Markdown, tables and main-content Markdown are all unchanged. Total time is 11.8 s against 10.6 s.
- **Well-formed tables.** In the Chromium position check from #121, 4,155 tables match with 0 differences.
- **Hard inputs** stay linear and of the same order as before:
  - 30,000 rows with a `<th>` in each cell;
  - 30,000 rows written in one cell;
  - 2,000 misnested tables;
  - 200 nested misnested tables;
  - 20,000 tables nested at table level: 995 ms, where 76530f0 threw `RangeError` after 57 s.
- **Review.** A subagent with a fresh context found three blocking problems on the first pass:
  - `<template>` rows became table rows;
  - a `<table>` at table level was moved before the table instead of ending it;
  - the CHANGELOG overstated the Chromium match.

  All three are fixed. A second review found nothing that blocks merging.
- `npm run typecheck`: exit 0.
- `npm test`: 145 files, 1,826 tests passed. This includes the two live-site tests.

## Known gaps (not fixed here)

- **A stray end tag after htmlparser2's own implied close** (fixed in #126). When a `<td>` or `<tr>` is written directly in a cell, htmlparser2 closes the cell (as browsers do). The cell's own `</td>` that follows is then stray. A browser ignores it, but htmlparser2 applies it to an outer cell that is still open. linkedom's tree has already lost the structure at that point, so the rebuild follows the tree. These are the 545 of 1,939 random tables above. I attribute all 545 to this cause because the count drops to 0 once those elements are wrapped, and I checked the smallest example by hand. Fixing it needs table-aware parsing at the token level, not a tree rebuild.
- **Formatting the reviewer saw differ (no effect on data tables):**
  - Chromium reopens `<b>` after a table written inside it at table level (`**y**` versus `y`).
  - When a misnested table sits in a cell of another table, the text after its inner table is placed differently.
- **Deep nesting.** Empty `<div>`s nested 10,000 deep at table level now overflow the call stack in the existing recursive walk. The same depth outside a table already overflowed before this change.

